<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Selyf ap Cynan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 615</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Selyf Sarffgadau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Selyf ap Cynan was a king of Powys in the early 7th century, known in Welsh tradition as Selyf Sarffgadau, 'the serpent of battles'. He was killed fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelfrith-of-northumbria">Æthelfrith of Northumbria</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chester">Battle of Chester</a> around 615, where, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, the English also slaughtered many monks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangor-on-dee">Bangor-on-Dee</a> who had come to pray for the British army.</p></div>
